# loop-fixpoint

The register a loop writes is also the register it reads, so proving it needs a FIXPOINT over the
loop body rather than one pass. Analyse this once and `sum` and `i` are `KTop` on the back edge and
nothing specializes; iterate to a fixpoint and all three arithmetic instructions are `i64`.

The conservative shortcut — "set every register the loop writes to unknown at its head" — is sound
and would leave this file entirely `dyn`, which is why the expectation is checked in.

```scalascript
def workload(): Int =
  var i = 0
  var sum = 0
  while i < 10 do
    sum = sum + i
    i = i + 1
  sum
```
